  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1046 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe 10/00Z H500 hand analysis and satellite imagery was showing \nthe Atlantic tropical system moving across the Florida Panhandle\nand the Pacific storm well off the central Baja coastline. \nOtherwise a fairly progressive pattern is in place across the \nlower 48 but this will change significantly over the next 24 to \n36 hours. This is due the main driver of the change which is a \nvery strong trough currently across the northern border in BC. \nThis trough will dig into the PacNW tomorrow and carve well off \nthe SoCal coast by early Sunday. This effectively puts strong \nSouthwest flow from the Baja Region across the 4 Corners into \nthe Northern High Plains. It should be no surprise then that \ncopious amounts of tropical moisture will be transported across \nthe Intermountain West beginning as early as tonight...but more \nrobust by tomorrow night. PWAT continues to surge to near an \ninch by midnight Sunday morning which will be near a \nclimatological maxima and at least in the 90th percentile or \nhigher. It will drop back some start next week but not really \ndry out until we head into the late week/weekend period. So a \nwet and cool forecast is on tap for much of this forecast with \ntomorrow and Sunday a transition period with temperatures warmer\nor closer to normal. Grabbing onto the finer details remains a \nchallenge over the next several days. How will cloud cover \naffect the convective potential...if convection what storm mode \nis more favorable...rainfall rates/flash flooding potential in \nthe stronger storms all remain in play. The next few afternoons \nCAMs all suggest some potential for low to possible moderate \nCAPE with the shear profile increasing through the weekend. \nThere will be jet support both days this weekend and the remnant\nenergy from Rachael moves through on Sunday providing extra \nfocus. Profiles on Sunday suggest storm mode supporting \norganized convection bringing a low probability (<15%) of bigger\nhail and wind gusts over 55 mph. This may be easy to achieve in\nthe 4 Corners where afternoon gusts may be well into 30s/low \n40s already. The skinny CAPE profile is also suggestive of warm \nrain processes and this will bring a flash flood threat with \nrainfall rates of 1-2 inches per hour. Things look to taper off \na bit on Monday as moisture decreases and hence CAPE decreases. \nShear however stays strong with a 100+kt jet overhead in the \ndownstream portion of the trough over the West. The forecast \nstill has 1-2 inches of rain over the eastern Utah mountain \nranges and the San Juans with a few peaks above 13K likely to \nget another dusting of snow. Confidence remains too low to put \nanything in a flood watch at this time. \n\nThe next system to impact the region is an early season winter storm \nas the cold-core Pacific Northwest low drops down through the Great \nbasin before lifting to the northeast across the region Tuesday \nevening and Wednesday. This won't bring a lot of snow to the region, \nbut we will likely see some winter driving conditions on the \nmountain passes with low visibilities and periods of slick roads. We \nwill likely see snow down to 8,000 ft, but don't expect any \naccumulation below about 10,000 ft. Stay tuned for updates on this \none too.",
